From 25b72a3767fa550d15b55eaec9f1702151ca31a6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 20:52:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] the audit trail says which project it changed, how bad it
 is, and whether anyone meant it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The trail recorded this, in full:

    ITK: status von Zustimmung erteilt auf offen gesetzt.

A granted approval was withdrawn and the record does not say on which of
1,298 projects. Nobody can act on it, nobody can audit it, and it cannot be
undone, because the entry does not know what it changed. Everything here
follows from that one sentence.

WHAT. shared/audit-entry.ts carries structure beside the prose: project id,
Projektnummer, Station, Gewerk, field, from, to, and the screen the change
was made on. The surface is read from the URL at write time rather than
threaded through applyEdit, applyReviewEdit, the wizard and the detail
dialog, which would be four chances to pass the wrong one. Entries written
before this file keep their prose and render exactly as they did.

HOW BAD. Withdrawing an approval and filling in a Prüfer's name used to be
identical rows, so finding the first meant reading all of the second.
Severity is now a function of consequence: an approval taken away, a
required check switched to "nicht erforderlich", or a project deleted are
kritisch; a renamed identity field or a newly blocked check is wichtig;
normal progress is routine. Status is read through the canonical
vocabulary, so "Niederschrift erstellt (LP05-05-01-F31)" grades the same as
its plain form. "Nur kritische" isolates them.

WAS IT MEANT. People mis-click. A change reversed ninety seconds later is
one person correcting themselves, not two decisions, so markCorrections
marks the earlier entry superseded inside a ten-minute window and the
default view stops it leading the page. It does not delete it: one click
shows every corrected entry, because a trail that can lose rows is not
evidence of anything. Inside the same window an entry offers Rückgängig,
which writes the old value back through the ordinary validated, optimistic,
audited mutation — the undo is a new entry, never an erasure.

Also: a stationary cursor no longer steals the search palette's keyboard
highlight. mouseenter fires when a list opens under a pointer that has not
moved, so Enter opened the row under the mouse rather than the row the
reader was looking at — which is also why one smoke assertion had been
failing intermittently across three runs. mousemove instead.
